he UK by the Collins Crime Club on 20 October 1930 and in the US by Dodd, Mead and Company later in the same year. The UK edition retailed at seven shillings and sixpence and the US edition at $2.00.", "newValue": "The Murder at the Vicarage, published on October 20, 1930, is a pivotal work in the mystery genre, written by British author Agatha Christie. Set in the fictional village of St. Mary Mead, it introduces the astute amateur detective Miss Marple, whose character would go on to become one of Christie's most beloved creations. This novel not only solidified Christie's reputation as the "Queen of Crime" but also played a significant role in shaping the trajectory of detective fiction in the 20th century, influencing writers and inspiring adaptations across various media.

The plot unfolds with the murder of the local vicar, Reverend Leonard Clement, discovered in his study. The investigation reveals underlying tensions among the villagers, uncovering secrets and motives that reflect the complexities of human relationships. Through a series of clever deductions and red herrings, Miss Marple ultimately solves the case, showcasing Christie's signature style of intricate plotting and psychological insight. The novel's unique blend of rural English life and the darker aspects of human nature marked a turning point in crime literature, transitioning from the more traditional forms to a focus on character-driven narratives.

